---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const b31 = requireSection('B3.1');
const b32 = requireSection('B3.2');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    B3 shifts programming from sequences of statements to models built from classes and objects. The
    chapter begins with single-class design, controlled state, and object construction, then extends
    at HL into class relationships, reusable hierarchies, and common design patterns.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Start by asking what one object should know and what it should do. Then decide how that object
      protects its state. At HL, extend that thinking to how several classes relate, when inheritance
      is justified, and when a pattern is useful rather than decorative.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b31}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.1.1"
    summary="Evaluate OOP by linking its modular strengths to realistic trade-offs, rather than describing it as automatically better than every other programming style."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Object-oriented programming organizes software around objects that combine data with behavior.
      In evaluation questions, the key point is judgement: OOP is powerful when a problem contains
      clear entities with state and actions, but it can add unnecessary structure for very small tasks.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-evaluating-oop-fundamentals">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Feature of OOP</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it helps</th>
          <th scope="col">Possible limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Modularity</td>
          <td>Classes separate responsibilities into manageable units</td>
          <td>Poor design can still produce tangled dependencies between classes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reusability</td>
          <td>Well-designed classes can be reused in later projects</td>
          <td>Reuse is weaker if the class is too specific to one scenario</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Encapsulation</td>
          <td>Objects control how their internal state changes</td>
          <td>Extra methods and access rules can feel heavy for trivial scripts</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Abstraction</td>
          <td>Complex behavior can be used through a simpler interface</td>
          <td>Too much abstraction can make a design harder to follow</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        OOP is not the same as “good programming” by itself. A weak class design can still be hard
        to test, hard to understand, and hard to maintain.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.1.2"
    summary="Construct class designs by showing class name, attributes, methods, and visibility clearly enough that the design can be translated into code."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A class design describes what an object stores and what it can do. In IB terms, this is usually
      represented as a UML class diagram. The essential skill is to design the class clearly, not to
      decorate the diagram with unnecessary detail.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-uml-class-design">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">UML part</th>
          <th scope="col">What to include</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Class name</td>
          <td>A singular noun naming the entity</td>
          <td><code>Book</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Attributes</td>
          <td>Stored data, often with visibility and type</td>
          <td><code>- title: String</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Methods</td>
          <td>Behaviors the object performs</td>
          <td><code>+ loan(): Boolean</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Visibility</td>
          <td>Access control for fields and methods</td>
          <td><code>+</code> public, <code>-</code> private</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-sketching-a-class-design">Worked example: sketching a class design</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-text">+-------------------------------+
| LibraryBook                   |
+-------------------------------+
| - title: String               |
| - isbn: String                |
| - isLoaned: Boolean           |
+-------------------------------+
| + loan(): Boolean             |
| + returnBook(): Void          |
| + getTitle(): String          |
+-------------------------------+</code></pre>
    <p>
      This design is strong because the attributes describe state, the methods describe behavior,
      and the visibility makes it clear which data should not be changed directly from outside.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.1.3, B3.1.4"
    summary="Distinguish between static and non-static members, then construct class code that defines a template and instantiates separate objects from it."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Static members belong to the class itself, while non-static members belong to each object
      instance. That distinction matters because some values should be shared across all objects,
      while others should stay unique to one object.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-static-vs-instance-members">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Member type</th>
          <th scope="col">Belongs to</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Static variable</td>
          <td>The class</td>
          <td>Shared counter, common constant, next available ID</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Instance variable</td>
          <td>One object</td>
          <td>Name, balance, speed, quantity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Static method</td>
          <td>The class</td>
          <td>Utility behavior that does not depend on one object's state</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Instance method</td>
          <td>One object</td>
          <td>Behavior that reads or updates that object's own data</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-defining-a-class-and-creating-objects">Worked example: defining a class and creating objects</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-python">class Product:
    next_id = 1000

    def __init__(self, name, price):
        self.name = name
        self.price = price
        self.product_id = Product.next_id
        Product.next_id += 1

    @staticmethod
    def is_valid_price(value):
        return value >= 0

keyboard = Product("Keyboard", 49.99)
mouse = Product("Mouse", 19.99)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Here, <code>next_id</code> is static because all objects share it, while
      <code>name</code>, <code>price</code>, and <code>product_id</code> belong to each separate
      object. Instantiation happens when the class is called to create <code>keyboard</code> and
      <code>mouse</code>.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.1.5"
    summary="Explain and apply encapsulation and information hiding so that an object manages its own state through controlled methods rather than unrestricted external changes."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Encapsulation means that the class controls access to its internal data. Information hiding is
      the related idea that outside code should not need to know the internal implementation details
      to use the class correctly.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-encapsulation-and-information-hiding">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Approach</th>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Private or protected data</td>
          <td>Reduces uncontrolled external access</td>
          <td>Prevents invalid state changes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Accessor method</td>
          <td>Reads data in a controlled way</td>
          <td>Keeps the interface clear without exposing implementation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mutator method with validation</td>
          <td>Updates data only when the rule is valid</td>
          <td>Protects integrity of the object state</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-python">class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, balance):
        self._balance = balance

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount > 0:
            self._balance += amount

    def get_balance(self):
        return self._balance</code></pre>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Important distinction">
      <p>
        Encapsulation does not mean data becomes magically unreachable in every language. The key
        IB idea is controlled access: the class decides how updates happen.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={b32}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.2.1, B3.2.2"
    summary="HL extends OOP into inheritance and polymorphism so related classes can reuse shared behavior and still respond differently through overriding."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Inheritance is appropriate when one class is a genuine specialization of another. Polymorphism
      then allows related objects to respond to the same method call in type-specific ways.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-inheritance-and-polymorphism">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Main idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Inheritance</td>
          <td>A child class reuses and extends parent behavior</td>
          <td><code>Bus</code> is a <code>Vehicle</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Superclass</td>
          <td>The more general parent class</td>
          <td><code>Vehicle</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Subclass</td>
          <td>The more specific child class</td>
          <td><code>Bus</code>, <code>Taxi</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Polymorphism</td>
          <td>One shared method name produces behavior based on runtime type</td>
          <td><code>describe()</code> called on mixed vehicle objects</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-python">class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        return "..."

class Dog(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        return "woof"

class Cat(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        return "meow"

animals = [Dog(), Cat(), Dog()]
outputs = [animal.speak() for animal in animals]</code></pre>
    <p>
      The loop uses one method call, <code>speak()</code>, but the result depends on the actual object
      type. That is the core of polymorphism through overriding.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Evaluation rule">
      <p>
        Inheritance should follow a real “is-a” relationship. If the only goal is code reuse, a
        different relationship such as composition may be a better fit.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.2.3, B3.2.4"
    summary="Abstraction defines what related classes must provide, while composition and aggregation explain two different kinds of HAS-A relationship between objects."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Abstraction focuses on essential behavior without forcing one concrete implementation in the
      parent design. Composition and aggregation then explain how objects can contain or depend on
      other objects in stronger or weaker ways.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-abstraction-composition-and-aggregation">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th scope="col">Representative example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Abstraction</td>
          <td>Defines essential behavior while hiding implementation detail</td>
          <td>An abstract <code>Shape</code> requires an <code>area()</code> method</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Composition</td>
          <td>A strong HAS-A relationship with dependent lifetime</td>
          <td>A <code>Car</code> composed of an <code>Engine</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Aggregation</td>
          <td>A weaker HAS-A relationship with independent lifetime</td>
          <td>A <code>Library</code> containing <code>Book</code> objects</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-composition-vs-aggregation">Worked example: composition vs aggregation</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Relationship</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>A playlist owns temporary play-history records</td>
          <td>Composition</td>
          <td>The record exists only as part of that playlist system</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A classroom stores references to student objects</td>
          <td>Aggregation</td>
          <td>Students still exist even if one classroom object is removed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A phone contains a battery created and managed with the phone</td>
          <td>Composition</td>
          <td>The part is tightly bound to the whole object's lifecycle</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="B3.2.5"
    summary="Explain common OOP design patterns by identifying the problem each pattern solves, not by memorizing large architecture catalogues."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A design pattern is a reusable design idea for a recurring problem. At this level, the important
      task is to explain what the pattern is for and when it is helpful.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-common-oop-design-patterns">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Pattern</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Simple example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Singleton</td>
          <td>Ensure only one instance of a class exists</td>
          <td>One shared configuration manager for an application</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Factory</td>
          <td>Create related objects through one construction point</td>
          <td>Building different notification objects from one request type</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Observer</td>
          <td>Notify dependent objects when one object changes</td>
          <td>Subscribers updating when a score or stock value changes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Scope boundary">
      <p>
        The chapter PDF briefly mentions interfaces when discussing some patterns, but the syllabus
        point here is the pattern purpose itself. Full interface syntax and wider architecture catalogs
        are outside the core IB focus for this chapter.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
